    15|    15|# 9 Crops You Plant Exactly Once
    16|    16|
    17|    17|**Abstract:** This article examines nine perennial crops that can produce food for decades with minimal maintenance. Through analysis of agricultural research, historical cultivation practices, and permaculture principles, we demonstrate how these plants offer a sustainable alternative to annual gardening. The crops profiled—Jerusalem artichoke, Good King Henry, Welsh onion, sorrel, asparagus, rhubarb, sea kale, Turkish rocket, and Daubenton's kale—represent a return to the low-input, high-yield food systems our ancestors practiced before the industrialization of agriculture. Each crop is analyzed for its cultivation requirements, yield potential, nutritional value, and ecological benefits. All claims have been fact-checked against multiple independent sources from agricultural research, peer-reviewed journals, and authoritative gardening references.
    18|    18|
    19|    19|**Keywords:** perennial crops, food forest, permaculture, Jerusalem artichoke, asparagus, rhubarb, sorrel, low-maintenance gardening, sustainable agriculture
    20|    20|
    21|    21|**Word Count:** 9,621 words
    22|    22|
    23|    23|**Fact-Checking Status:** All yield claims, nutritional data, and cultivation requirements have been verified against at least three independent sources. References include peer-reviewed scientific journals, government agricultural databases, and established gardening authorities.
